lds and a pair. Let's see: the existing meld is a Chi of T4, T5, T6. If I can form a Pung of J1 (but I have two), so I need one more. Or a pair of J1 and another meld. But without more melds, it's hard to reach the required points.\n\nGiven the valid actions, I need to choose one. Let's consider the options:\n\n- Play B1: There are 2 left. Safe to discard.\n- Play T1: 4 left. Safe.\n- Play T5: 1 left. Risky if others need it.\n- Play B2: 3 left. Safe.\n- Play J3: 3 left. Safe.\n- Play J2: 1 left. Very risky.\n- Play W2: 4 left. Safe.\n- Play J1: 2 left. Safe.\n- Play W1: 3 left. Safe.\n\nThe most risky ones are T5 and J2. So I should avoid those. The best options are to discard a tile with more copies left. So B1, T1, B2, W2, J1, W1, J3. Among these, which is least useful for forming melds?\n\nLooking at the suited tiles, B1 and B2 are Dots. If I discard B1, maybe someone can form a Chi with B1, B2, B3. But I have B2 in hand. If I discard B1, they might need B2 and B3. But the unshown B3 has 2 left. So it's possible. Similarly, discarding T1 might help someone form T1, T2, T3. The unshown T2 has 2 left. So if I discard T1, someone might have T2 and T3 to form a Chi. But I have T1 in hand, so maybe I need to keep it for my own Chi.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W1 or W2, they might be part of a Chi. For example, W1, W2, W3. The unshown W3 has 3 left. So if I discard W1, someone might have W2 and W3 to form a Chi. But I have W2 in hand. If I discard W2, they might need W1 and W3. The unshown W1 has 3 left. So it's possible.\n\nConsidering all this, perhaps the safest tile to discard is J3, since it's an honor tile and there are three left. Discarding J3 is less likely to help others form a meld. Alternatively, discarding one of the suited tiles that's part of a possible Chi, but since I don't have the others, it's better to discard a tile that's not useful for my hand.\n\nLooking at my hand, I have W1, W2, T1, T5, B1, B2. These are all singles. If I can form any meld